1. Abstract
Customer churn is an important business problem for subscription-based services. This project uses data analytics and machine learning to understand churn patterns and predict whether a customer is likely to leave. The workflow includes data cleaning, exploratory data analysis, visualization, classification models and AI-driven insights. The dataset is an original synthetic educational dataset created for this project.
2. Problem Statement
The objective is to identify factors associated with customer churn and build a predictive model that can flag customers who may be at higher risk of leaving. Such predictions can help a business prioritize retention efforts.
3. Objectives
Clean and prepare customer data.
Perform exploratory data analysis (EDA).
Identify important churn patterns using visualizations.
Build classification models for churn prediction.
Evaluate models using Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score and ROC-AUC.
Generate practical AI-driven retention insights.
4. Dataset Description
The dataset contains 1,200 customer records and 15 input attributes covering demographics, tenure, services, contract and billing. The target variable is Churn.
The dataset is synthetic and contains no real customer or personally identifiable information.
5. Data Cleaning and Preprocessing
Converted charge fields to numeric format.
Filled missing numerical values using median imputation.
Filled missing categorical values using mode imputation.
Created ChurnFlag as the binary target.
Applied one-hot encoding to categorical variables.
Standardized numerical variables for Logistic Regression.
Used a stratified 80/20 train-test split.
